Each polarization element has been used for single-molecule orientation measurements: excitation cycled among three linear polarizations~\cite{Backer2016}, emission split into four polarization channels~\cite{Rimoli2022}, and excitation-polarization modulation combined with polarization-resolved detection~\cite{Beausang2013}.
We show analytically that linearly polarized excitation enhances the counting contrast for any emitter orientations, and that polarization-resolved detection adds count information through the allocation of photons among channels in multiphoton pulses. Maximum-likelihood simulations for two to five emitters of unknown orientations and brightnesses confirm these predictions: excitation polarization substantially reduces the required pulse budget, whereas emission polarization helps mainly in combination with it, increasingly so as the number of emitters grows for equal amplitudes and tilted dipoles.

