Alongside these developments, another line of research has focused on representing catalogue items through discrete codes drawn from shared codebooks. Vector and product quantization have been used to construct item representations for sequential recommendation~\cite{hou2023vqrec,petrov2024recjpq}. In generative retrieval, TIGER instead uses content-derived semantic IDs (SIDs) as autoregressive prediction targets~\cite{rajput2023tiger}. Subsequent work has refined identifier construction by incorporating language and collaborative signals, semantic tree structures, and diverse code assignments~\cite{zheng2024lcrec,si2024seater,wang2024letter}. More recently, ETEGRec and DIGER have connected identifier learning more closely to recommendation optimization~\cite{liu2025etegrec,fu2026diger}, while ActionPiece explores context-dependent tokenization of action sequences~\cite{hou2025actionpiece}. Given such identifiers, a separate modeling choice concerns how their code positions are predicted.

