\subsection{The value of smoother predictors}\label{sec:preferredexponent}

For the Mat\'ern family $\nu_s<1/2$, decreasing $\nu_s$ makes the predictor smoother. We compare the best value at each exponent by optimizing its decay rate:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:exponentvalue}
\bar v^\star(\nu_s)=\sup_{\theta_s>0}v^\star(\nu_s,\theta_s).
\end{equation}
Value is nondecreasing with smoothness under this comparison. Hence the supremum over the family is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:preferredexponent}
\sup_{\nu_s<1/2,\,\theta_s>0}v^\star(\nu_s,\theta_s)
=\lim_{\nu_s\to-\infty}\bar v^\star(\nu_s).
\end{equation}
The ordering applies across admissible friction symbols; fixing the decay rate instead can reverse the ordering in the exposure-bleeding regime. For ordinary holdings with inventory risk, $\beta=0$, $\lambda>0$, the impact models considered here give a stronger comparison: smoother predictors have no lower value even at a fixed decay rate. Optimizing the rate gives the same supremum $s_\alpha^2/(2\lambda)$ for every exponent, approached by slow signals.

