	\section{Introduction}
	\label{sec:introduction}
		The Hubbard model is a widely used minimal effective description of correlated electrons in molecules and materials.
		Combining a tight-binding hopping term with a purely on-site interaction, it captures essential phenomena observed in nature, such as the transition from a semimetal to an antiferromagnetic Mott insulator \cite{ostmeyer_semimetalmott_2020,ostmeyer_antiferromagnetic_2021}.
		Its simplicity has made it particularly popular for the description of low-dimensional carbon-based systems, such as perylene \cite{rodekamp_single-particle_2025}, carbon nanotubes \cite{Luu:2015gpl}, and most prominently graphene \cite{Brower:2011nqf}.

		However, despite its success, the Hubbard model neglects a number of effects present in real materials, and extending it in different directions gives rise to a considerably richer variety of physics.
