The chain ends when the host galaxy leaves the cavity it is feeding. The AGN separates from its cavity through two independent motions: the host galaxy's drift through the halo gas, and the buoyant rise of the bubble out of its own cavity. The host drift speed is set by the virial velocity dispersion of eq.~\eqref{eq:sigmav},
\begin{equation}
v_{\rm drift} = f_{v{\rm AGN}} \sigma_v,
\qquad
\sigma_v = \sqrt{\frac{k_{\rm B}T}{\mu m_p}},
\end{equation}
with $f_{v{\rm AGN}}=1$ for satellites and $f_{v{\rm AGN,cen}}=0.3$ for centrals; the three velocity components are drawn from a Maxwellian with per-axis dispersion $f_{v{\rm AGN}}\sigma_v$. A buoyant bubble rises out of its cavity at a fraction of the ambient sound speed \citep{churazov_evolution_2001},
\begin{equation}
v_{\rm buoy} = f_{\rm buoy} c_s,
\qquad
c_s = \sqrt{\frac{\gamma_{\rm gas} k_{\rm B}T}{\mu m_p}},
\end{equation}
with $\gamma_{\rm gas}=5/3$ and $f_{\rm buoy}=1.0$. Because drift and buoyant rise act in independent directions, the effective separation speed adds them in quadrature,
\begin{equation}
v_{\rm AGN} = \sqrt{v_{\rm drift}^2 + v_{\rm buoy}^2},
\label{eq:vagn}
\end{equation}
