\subsection{What the algorithms do}\label{sec:algorithms}

Every tensor network algorithm either optimizes the tensors for a variational target or evolves them in real or imaginary time, and both rest on contraction, the evaluation of the network (Fig.~\ref{fig:operations}). Optimization finds the tensors that minimize the energy. DMRG does so by sweeping through the chain and solving an effective eigenvalue problem, of dimension set by $\chi$, at each site while the rest of the network is held fixed \cite{White1992,Schollwock2011}; the same sweep structure is used for trees, and gradient-based and automatically differentiated optimization has made variational PEPS practical \cite{Corboz2016,Liao2019,Naumann2025}. Evolution applies $e^{-iHt}$ or $e^{-\beta H}$ to a state, either by splitting the Hamiltonian into local gates and truncating after each layer, which is the time-evolving block decimation (TEBD) \cite{Vidal2004tebd}, or by projecting the exact evolution onto the tangent space of the ansatz, which is the time-dependent variational principle (TDVP) \cite{Haegeman2011,Haegeman2016,Paeckel2019}. Real time gives dyn

