Here are some rules of thumb for determining the adequacy of the translations provided by a commercial translation tool (or of any translations):
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep, topsep=0pt]
\item{} Use Multidimensional Quality Metric (MQM) \citep{mqm} to define what  \textit{major errors} might exist. Ultimately, there should be no \textit{major errors}, but minor errors may be acceptable.\footnote{Examples of major errors may include incorrectly translated safety instructions, incorrect locations, or incorrect times.}
\item{} Critical data, such as place names, should be kept in the original language because geographic place names tend to stay consistent and do not change based on language spoken. This information can be evaluated even by those who do not read the target language.
\end{itemize}


