\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We built news topics from an encoder that has read nothing published after 1995 and tested them against readers trained on recent text, in a design where every model choice precedes the test period. The point-in-time encoder gave stable topics and forecasts comparable to the best alternative. Measuring attention shocks over a quarter improved forecasts for every reader, and combining readers and models produced the strongest portfolios, with a monthly net Sharpe ratio of 1.12. That result is significant against zero before adjusting for the search, but it does

