{\bf Introduction:} A clear signature of the strongly correlated nature of the fractional quantum Hall effect (FQHE) is that its ``fundamental particles" are composite fermions (CFs)~\cite{Jain89,Jain07,Jain20}, topologically distinct from electrons. Much of the vast phenomenology of the FQHE is straightforwardly understandable in terms of noninteracting CFs: the observed sequences $\nu=n/(2pn\pm 1)$ as integer quantum Hall effect (IQHE) of CFs~\cite{Jain89} and the non-FQH state at half-filling as a Fermi liquid metal of CFs~\cite{Halperin93,Halperin20,Shayegan20}.  In particular, when an electron is added to a FQHE state, it splits into many fractionally charged CFs. 

The CF paradigm has also suggested explicit wave functions. The construction proceeds through the following steps, taking the example of the incompressible ground state at $\nu=n/(2pn+1)$: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item begin with the known IQHE state $\Phi_n$ at filling factor $n$;
    \item attach vortices to each electron by multiplication by a Jastrow factor $\prod_{j<k}(z_j-z_k)^{2p}\sim \Phi_1^{2p}$ where $z_j=x_j-iy_j$ represents the position of the $j$th electron as a complex quantity; and 
    \item project the product into the LLL to obtain a wave function residing strictly in the LLL, as appropriate in the limit of high magnetic fields.
\end{enumerate}
This produces the Jain states $\Psi_{\nu=n/(2pn\pm 1)}={\cal P}_{\rm LLL} \Phi_n \Phi_1^{2p}$. (Multiplication by $\Phi_1^{2p}$ followed by LLL projection is referred to as composite-fermionization.)
Wave functions for charged and neutral excitations can be similarly constructed by beginning with the charged and neutral excitations of the IQHE state. These have been found to be exceedingly accurate in comparisons to exact eigenstates known for systems containing up to 16-18 particles~\cite{Jain07}.

